Please don't know this test can't work if the assumption is wrong. There is no mechanism for asserting the assumption. Please correct me if I am wrong here. 

A: What you have written is not a test but partial contract or code coverage test. Pex generates a lot of test cases based on the pre-condition but it cannot cover every possible path of real application behavior. 

It's always good to unit test individual methods and scenarios, not only the contract. But the Contract Testing is just a kind of it. If you want to fully test the boundary condition and the corner case of your algorithm, you can use Pex to generate some test cases for the unit test.

In your case, LastIndexOf method will return a value between 0 and Count-1, so your assertion can cover about 80% of possible paths. What if the value you want to find is not in the array, or it is the first one, the last one, or even duplicate ones? The test will make sure your method can go through all these corner cases.

For example, assume you have an array {5, 3, 6, 3, 7}, lastIndexOf(3) will either return 1 or 3. Testing these two cases can ensure your code goes through all these conditions.

Remember, these assertions are not about the rightness of the code, they are just about the completeness of the test case. If the assertions can cover the real-world use scenarios well, that would be a good sign that your test is good.

So, to sum up, these tests are not for finding wrong code, but for covering the broad areas of the code base in a systematic way when real-world data is likely to be encountered.


A: You are correct in recognizing that PexContract tests can help to prove that the contracts are met but they are not enough to validate the correctness of your code. Code coverage tools and unit tests are always important and should be used together.

A: PEX generates sets of tests based on your provided arguments and preconditions. These tests are meant to validate your contracts and are not meant to provide an exhaustive validation for your application flow.

For instance, your LastIndexOf method has been provided an ArrayList and an integer x. The contract is that LastIndexOf will return the last index of x. The PEX contract will cover corner cases - when the x exists in the List, it occurs at the start, end or duplicated. It covers these and much more. 

However, PEX, like all testing tools, should not replace the need to test edge cases or more complex data scenarios in an exhaustive fashion. You should still consider testing the more common conditions and scenarios. Also, always remember to test a wide variety of data inputs, including those at the edge of the specified data range. These test cases may not be covered by PEX, and so should be included in your comprehensive testing.

In your case, an empty list is also being tested which is not covered by PEX's contract. Therefore it's crucial to conduct your own exploratory tests using diverse data sets.

So, in summary, the PEX Contract Test is a good start but should be complemented with much more comprehensive testing.


A: PEX and similar automated testing tools are there to assist in providing a "first line of defense" against bugs. However, they are not a replacement for thorough manual testing or code review practices. 
If the test shows you that an assertion fires, then the test is telling you that there's potentially a bug, but it doesn't demonstrate that you have a bug. You would then have to write a test or perform a manual check to see if the bug exists, and what edge cases should you test to isolate the bug.

A: The purpose of a contract test like this one is to ensure that the public contract of your method, based on the precondition and postcondition, is maintained even when new behavior is added in the implementation of your method. 
In your test, you assume that LastIndexOf(x) is already correctly implemented in the ArrayList class and you only check whether the return value is within the acceptable range. Your assumptions might not be correct in a specific situation where ArrayList class doesn't handle LastIndexOf operation correctly. 
So, while PexContract test is helping to verify if your new behavior correctly fits in with the public contract, you still need to use other tests to verify the correctness of--------------------	    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(New_Ret < 0 || New_arrListContainsX && New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);

            // PEX: The value returned from the method is >=0 and <= the new count of elements in the array list
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= -1 && New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);

            // PEX: The array list count does not change
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

            // PEX: The position of the old item index is <= new index
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

```

#### Failing Tests

Now, you should see some failing unit tests. 

```
[Test]
public void PUT_Add_Contract([PexMethod under test here])
{
    //...
    Assert.Inconclusive("Pex hasn't found enough test cases.");
}
```

These tests are finding multiple ways to add an item to a list whose size is never exceeded. In other words, this is a sound invariant that should be satisfied.

These tests cover a wide range of potential scenarios and thus do not miss any edge cases. Running these should not return an inconclusive error, as they represent a set of well-defined test cases.

